## Educational Philippine Folklore Field Notes & Neighbor Dossiers for the Karimlan Archive.
## Each neighbor unlocks 6 progressive chapters across Reputation Ranks 1–6:
##   Rank 1: Identity & Regional Origins
##   Rank 2: Traditional Beliefs, Customs & Taboos
##   Rank 3: Appearance, Traits & Encounters (+ Portrait Archival Illustration & Artist Credit)
##   Rank 4: Etymology & Historical Roots
##   Rank 5: Lesser-Known Variations & Rare Trivia
##   Rank 6: Living Tradition & Modern Legacy

const ENTRIES: Dictionary = {
	"kuyakap": {
		"creature": "Kapre",
		"region": "Nationwide (Luzon, Visayas & Bicol)",
		"portrait": "res://Assets/characters/portraits/Kuya_Kap Face.png",
		"rank1_origin": "In Philippine folklore, the kapre is a nocturnal, tree-dwelling giant most often described as dark-skinned, muscular, and smoking a large cigar atop big trees such as the balete, acacia, or fruitless mango (Maximo Ramos, 1971; Carlos \"Botong\" Francisco, 1955). One of the earliest printed English accounts comes from American folklorist Fletcher Gardner in 1906, who recorded in Tagalog tradition: \"Another monster, but a benign one, is the kapre. It is gigantic in size, being even larger than the tik-balan, and is perfectly black.\"\n\nUnlike predatory figures such as the aswang, folk accounts generally characterize the kapre as mischievous rather than inherently evil. He is commonly portrayed as an omnivorous, solitary being who sits in the branches watching passersby, though oral tradition warns that he can turn vengeful if his home tree is cut down.",
		"rank2_taboos": "Traditional precautions around large, old trees reflect the broader Filipino custom of acknowledging unseen spirits through the polite phrase \"tabi-tabi po\" (\"excuse me\"), documented in ethnographic studies of Philippine supernatural beliefs (Vicerra & Javier, MANUSYA; Michael L. Tan, 2008). Cutting down a tree believed to house a kapre without permission is traditionally avoided for fear of provoking retaliation.\n\nRegional traditions also record specific protections and remedies against tree-dwelling giants. In Visayan lore regarding the closely related agta, folklorist Francisco Demetrio's Encyclopedia of Philippine Folk Beliefs and Customs notes the practice of carrying a bottle of mercury (asugui) as protection. Other folk accounts describe kapres disorienting travelers on familiar paths or approaching humans simply to form friendships or court mortal women they admire.",
		"rank3_depiction_path": "res://Assets/ui/journal/kapre.jpg",
		"rank3_depiction_orientation": "portrait",
		"rank3_depiction_source": "Illustration Source: Myth and Folklore Wiki (Fandom) — Kapre",
		"rank3_depiction_url": "https://mythus.fandom.com/wiki/Kapre?file=Kapre.jpg",
		"rank3_body": "Oral tradition describes the kapre as a towering, dark-skinned, muscular giant with thick body hair and a beard, often dressed in a loincloth (bahag). He inhabits massive old trees—most commonly balete, acacia, and mango—and occasionally deserted structures. Zambales folklore recorded by Maximo D. Ramos (1971) adds that he can shift in size from a small manikin to a giant as tall as a church tower, with glassy eyes as large as plates.\n\nTravelers typically recognize a kapre's presence before spotting him. Encounters begin with the sudden smell of burning tobacco, the glow of a cigar as thick as a man's thigh, or fireflies attributed to its falling embers. Able to turn invisible, he announces himself by shaking branches on windless nights, making bird-like chirping sounds (Ramos, 1971), laughing from the canopy, or disorienting passersby on familiar trails.",
		"rank4_history": "The name \"kapre\" derives from the Spanish word cafre (itself traced to the Arabic kafir, meaning \"unbeliever\"), which colonial authorities applied to dark-skinned African peoples and Papuan slaves brought to the Philippines during the colonial era. In the 1860 edition of Juan de Noceda and Pedro de Sanlúcar's Vocabulario de la lengua tagala, \"Cafre\" is still defined strictly in human terms as a person born on the coast of Africa (\"Tauong bocal ó tubo sa baybayin nang África\"), with no mention of a cigar-smoking tree spirit.\n\nFolklorists and researchers note that the modern national image of the kapre emerged as this colonial label merged with older regional traditions of dark, tree-associated beings—such as the Ilokano pugot, which Isabelo de los Reyes noted in El Folk-Lore Filipino (1889) some Spaniards called \"cafre.\" Tobacco itself was introduced from the Americas during Spanish rule, anchoring the cigar motif firmly in the colonial period.",
		"rank5_trivia": "Rather than representing a single uniform pre-colonial myth, modern folklore research suggests \"kapre\" became an umbrella term that absorbed traits from distinct regional beings (Maximo Ramos, 1971; Jordan Clark, The Aswang Project). In the Visayas, dictionaries and ethnographies by John U. Wolff (1972) and F. Landa Jocano (1983) document the agta—a dark, tree- and swamp-dwelling giant closely paralleled with the kapre. In Bohol, a 1950 road-accident account preserved in regional folklore even led locals to post warning signs reading \"Agta! Blow your horn\" along a highway embankment.\n\nLesser-known folk accounts recorded by Ramos also mention kapres making bird-like chirping sounds from the canopy, and a widespread motif holds that a kapre possesses a magical white stone; if a human manages to obtain it, the giant is said to grant wishes.",
		"rank6_epilogue": "Over the twentieth and twenty-first centuries, the kapre became one of the most recognizable figures in Philippine cinema, television, and print media. Documented screen appearances range from Eddie Romero's epic fantasy film Kamakalawa (1982) to horror and drama works such as Ang Pamana: The Inheritance (2006), Alvin Yapan's Ang Panggagahasa kay Fe (2009), and the 2022 Hulu anthology episode \"The Kapre\" in Bite Size Halloween.\n\nEven outside fiction, the figure endures in everyday Filipino culture as a familiar symbol of respect for old trees and rural landscapes. Whether referenced jokingly when passing a sprawling acacia at night or studied by contemporary folklore researchers unpacking its colonial and regional roots, the kapre remains an active part of Philippine oral heritage."
	},
	"manangana": {
		"creature": "Manananggal",
		"region": "Western Visayas (Capiz, Iloilo, Antique) & Bicol",
		"portrait": "res://Assets/characters/portraits/ManangAna Face.png",
		"rank1_origin": "The manananggal is one of the most iconic and feared creatures in Philippine folklore, classified by folklorist Maximo D. Ramos (1971) as a \"viscera sucker\" within the broader aswang tradition. By day, she lives undetected as an ordinary member of the community; by night, she detaches her upper torso from the waist and takes flight in search of sleeping victims, particularly pregnant women and their unborn children.\n\nAlthough the name itself is Tagalog, oral traditions of self-segmenting nocturnal predators span the archipelago—most vividly across the Western Visayas (Capiz, Iloilo, and Antique) and the Bicol region. Across these provinces, the creature embodies deep-seated rural anxieties over nocturnal vulnerability, sudden illness, and the perils of childbirth.",
		"rank2_taboos": "Traditional household precautions focus on guarding expectant mothers and infants after sundown. Families hang cloves of garlic, scatter rock salt, or place sharp objects, vinegar, and stingray tail whips (buntot pagi) near windows, doorways, and beneath the bed—items widely held in folk belief to repel or injure the creature.\n\nIf a manananggal is suspected of hunting nearby, oral tradition offers a decisive countermeasure: search secluded banana groves or thickets for her abandoned lower torso. Folk belief holds that packing the exposed waist with rock salt, crushed garlic, or ash—or moving the legs far from their original spot—prevents the upper body from reattaching, causing the creature to perish when the sun rises.",
		"rank3_depiction_path": "res://Assets/ui/journal/manananggal.jpg",
		"rank3_depiction_orientation": "portrait",
		"rank3_depiction_source": "Artwork: \"Mananaggal\" by isaiahpaul (DeviantArt)",
		"rank3_depiction_url": "https://www.deviantart.com/isaiahpaul/art/Mananaggal-113488304",
		"rank3_body": "Oral lore describes the manananggal by day as an unassuming or even attractive woman (Ramos, 1971). After nightfall, she anoints herself with a foul-smelling oil, sprouts leathery, bat-like wings, and splits cleanly at the waist, leaving her standing lower half hidden on the ground while her upper torso rises into the dark.\n\nHunting from the rooftops of nipa huts or quiet houses, she unfurls a long, thread-like proboscis tongue through tiny gaps in the rafters to siphon blood and internal organs from sleeping victims. Rural residents recognize her approach by the flapping of heavy wings or the rhythmic \"tik-tik\" and \"wak-wak\" calls of night birds—warnings that folklore insists grow fainter the closer the predator gets.",
		"rank4_history": "The name \"manananggal\" comes from the Tagalog root tanggal (\"to remove\" or \"to separate\"), literally meaning \"one who separates itself.\" Early Spanish accounts document closely related traditions: in 1589, Fray Juan de Plasencia wrote in Customs of the Tagalogs of the magtatangal, noting that \"his purpose was to show himself at night to many persons, without his head or entrails.\" By 1703, Fray Domingo de los Santos's Vocabulario de la lengua tagala explicitly defined magtatangal as a flying witch who detaches half its body and leaves the other half at home.\n\nFolklorists note strong regional kinship with neighboring Southeast Asian self-segmenting spirits—such as Malaysia's penanggalan and Thailand's krasue—pointing to shared archipelagic roots long before colonial contact, later shaped by Spanish-era witch lore and local fears surrounding maternal and infant mortality.",
		"rank5_trivia": "While modern pop culture almost universally portrays the manananggal as female, historical ethnography reveals that separating viscera suckers were not always women. Ferdinand Blumentritt (1895) and Maximo D. Ramos (1971) documented non-gendered and explicitly male counterparts—including the iqui of Batangas, a male flyer who leaves his lower body at home and attacks from rooftops with a nearly invisible tongue, as well as a male manananggal recorded in Bicol.\n\nEarly written accounts also show that the creature's anatomy was not always fixed. Plasencia's 1589 report and Blumentritt's nineteenth-century Visayan entries describe variations where only the head and entrails detached, showing that the now-standard bat-winged, waist-severed silhouette crystallized only over subsequent centuries.",
		"rank6_epilogue": "The manananggal holds a foundational place in Philippine cinema and media history. José Nepomuceno's lost 1927 silent film Ang Manananggal—starring Mary Walter—is widely regarded as the first Filipino horror movie, while Peque Gallaga's celebrated \"Manananggal\" segment in Shake, Rattle & Roll (1984) defined the creature's dramatic winged transformation for generations of audiences.\n\nToday, the figure has expanded far beyond campfire scares and horror anthologies. Contemporary Filipino and diasporic poets, filmmakers, and illustrators frequently reclaim the manananggal as a potent metaphor for colonial history, bodily autonomy, and marginalized femininity—ensuring that one of the archipelago's oldest night terrors remains an enduring voice in modern Filipino storytelling."
	},
	"reynamayari": {
		"creature": "Dalaketnon",
		"region": "Eastern Visayas (Samar & Leyte)",
		"portrait": "res://Assets/characters/portraits/ReynaMayari Face.png",
		"rank1_origin": "The dalaketnon (recorded as dalakitnon in the Eastern Visayas) are tall, fair-skinned tree spirits of Waray folklore who belong to the wider Philippine engkanto tradition. Folklorist Maximo D. Ramos provides one of the rare written ethnographic accounts of them in The Creatures of Philippine Lower Mythology (1971 / 1990), glossing their name as \"those who live in the balete tree.\"\n\nUnlike monstrous predators such as the aswang, the dalaketnon appear aristocratic, refined, and virtually indistinguishable from wealthy mortals. Rather than remaining isolated in the wilderness, oral lore remembers them as cosmopolitan spirit people who slip seamlessly into human towns—dancing at village halls, attending college, and traveling abroad—while their true homes remain hidden inside ancient forest trees.",
		"rank2_taboos": "In Maximo D. Ramos's brief ethnographic record, no formal charms, prayers, or rituals are documented specifically to ward off a dalaketnon. Instead, practical caution draws from broader Eastern Visayan engkanto etiquette: remaining quiet and respectful when passing massive old balete (dalakit) trees—especially at dusk or along remote riverbanks—to avoid drawing unwanted attention from their unseen households.\n\nWider Visayan oral tradition surrounding tree-dwelling engkantos also warns strongly against accepting invitations to lavish otherworldly feasts. A well-known regional taboo—commonly linked to both the dalaketnon and Samar's enchanted city of Biringan—cautions travelers never to eat black rice offered by spirit hosts, as tasting their food is believed to bind a mortal to the spirit realm forever.",
		"rank3_depiction_path": "res://Assets/ui/journal/dalaketnon.jpg",
		"rank3_depiction_orientation": "portrait",
		"rank3_depiction_source": "Artwork: \"Creatura 2: The Book of Long Ears\" by Ian Sta. Maria (Instagram)",
		"rank3_depiction_url": "https://www.instagram.com/p/Dbuj5JdCHXC/",
		"rank3_body": "Traditional East Visayan accounts recorded by Maximo D. Ramos describe both male and female dalaketnon as tall and strikingly handsome, with smooth white skin, wavy brown hair, and fine clothes woven with gold and silver thread. To mortal eyes, their dwellings deep in the wild woods appear as ordinary balete (strangler fig) trees, yet inside they are grand, bustling mansions.\n\nTravelers rarely spot a dalaketnon's true home directly, recognizing it instead by sensory clues drifting out of an empty forest: the clinking of dishes, the aroma of food frying, or the faint crying of a baby coming from inside a hollow tree. Their defining supernatural gift is illusion—making a forest appear as a splendid city for a single night, or passing unnoticed as a charming stranger, classmate, or beauty queen.",
		"rank4_history": "The name \"dalaketnon\" comes from dalakit (also dakit), the Visayan name for the balete or strangler fig tree, combined with the suffix -non (\"of\" or \"from\")—literally meaning \"people of the dalakit tree.\" Linguists note that it is distinct from dalaguetenon, the Cebuano term for residents of Dalaguete.\n\nAlthough reverence for spirit-inhabited trees has deep pre-colonial roots across the archipelago, the dalaketnon's fair-skinned, gold-clad, and car-driving image reflects centuries of colonial and modern evolution. Like the broader engkanto concept—derived from the Spanish encanto (\"enchantment\")—their appearance mirrors historical visions of colonial elites and modern urban wealth, turning an ancient forest-spirit belief into a reflection of social aspiration and the uncanny allure of distant cities.",
		"rank5_trivia": "The single full tale recorded by Maximo D. Ramos tells of a city woman summering in a rural village who falls for a handsome dalakitnon at a local dance. When he drives her to meet his family, she beholds a dazzling city of wide streets and fine houses—only to be found weeping alone in the forest the next morning.\n\nThat motif places the dalaketnon in the same Eastern Visayan folklore landscape as Biringan, Samar's legendary invisible city of engkantos. While Biringan is not named in Ramos's dalakitnon entry or Francisco Demetrio's 1991 encyclopedia, Samar oral traditions (traced to at least the 1960s) share striking parallels: massive balete trees in towns like Pagsanghan acting as portals, alluring spirit hosts offering forbidden black rice, and visitors waking beneath the same trees as though from a dream.",
		"rank6_epilogue": "Because written records of the dalaketnon were originally brief, modern Philippine pop culture played a major role in reshaping their nationwide image. In ABS-CBN's hit fantasy series Da Adventures of Pedro Penduko (2006) and Pedro Penduko at ang mga Engkantao (2007), the dalaketnon were reimagined as elitist, gothic-styled rulers of evil engkantos wielding telekinesis and corporeal duplication—traits that later spread widely across online wikis and fan lore.\n\nToday, folklorists and Filipino creatives increasingly distinguish these vivid television inventions from the original East Visayan oral tradition. Alongside Samar's enduring Biringan legend—which continues to inspire contemporary Filipino literature and cinema—the dalaketnon remain a captivating reminder of how Philippine tree-spirit lore adapts to every new era."
	},
	"rosalyn": {
		"creature": "Kaperosa",
		"region": "Western Visayas (Panay & Negros) & Luzon",
		"portrait": "res://Assets/characters/portraits/Rosalyn Face.png",
		"rank1_origin": "The kaperosa (also spelled caperosa) is a hooded, white-robed female apparition in Philippine ghost lore, most widely remembered in Western Visayas and older Tagalog oral traditions. Unlike pre-colonial nature spirits such as the diwata or engkanto, the kaperosa belongs to folk-Catholic beliefs surrounding restless human souls—known as kalag in the Visayas and multo in Luzon.\n\nWritten ethnographic records dedicated solely to the kaperosa are relatively sparse compared to figures like the aswang or kapre. Folklorists such as Maximo D. Ramos group similar shrouded apparitions within broader Philippine ghost traditions, where the figure serves as a lingering colonial-era precursor to the modern Filipino \"White Lady.\"",
		"rank2_taboos": "Because oral tradition regards the kaperosa as a wandering spirit rather than a territorial nature deity, traditional precautions draw heavily from Filipino folk Catholicism. Travelers passing old churches, cemeteries, or lonely crossroads after dark are advised to make the Sign of the Cross, whisper prayers such as the Hail Mary, or carry a rosary or blessed palm (palaspas) for protection.\n\nElders also warn against staring directly beneath the apparition's hood or responding if she silently beckons. In communities where a kaperosa is repeatedly sighted, families often respond not with hostility, but by lighting candles and offering a Mass (pamisa) so the troubled soul may find peace—especially during Kalag-kalag or Undas.",
		"rank3_depiction_path": "res://Assets/ui/journal/kaperosa.jpg",
		"rank3_depiction_orientation": "portrait",
		"rank3_depiction_source": "Artwork: \"White Lady, a.k.a. the Kaperosa\" by oriontaking (Instagram)",
		"rank3_depiction_url": "https://www.instagram.com/p/DQVlUH0kX_B/",
		"rank3_body": "Oral accounts describe the kaperosa as a tall, slender female figure draped from head to toe in a long white hooded cloak or burial shroud. Her face is usually hidden in deep shadow beneath the cowl, though some eyewitnesses report catching a glimpse of a pale, gaunt, or featureless visage. She rarely speaks, communicating instead through an eerie, motionless presence.\n\nEncounters typically occur late at night along deserted country lanes, sugar cane fields, bridges, or beside centuries-old stone churches and graveyards. Witnesses recognize her by a sudden drop in temperature, the faint scent of melted candle wax or damp earth, and the unsettling sight of her gliding silently inches above the ground before vanishing into the dark.",
		"rank4_history": "The name \"kaperosa\" comes directly from the Spanish word caperuza, meaning a pointed hood or hooded cape. This linguistic root places the tradition firmly within the Spanish colonial period (16th to 19th centuries), when Catholic funerary shrouds, hooded Lenten penitent robes, and teachings about Purgatory deeply shaped local beliefs about the afterlife.\n\nOver generations, the Spanish term merged with existing Filipino concepts of lingering ancestral and restless souls. While pre-colonial communities feared malevolent spirits of the dead returning to harm the living, the colonial kaperosa reflected a syncretic worldview—one where tragic death, unavenged wrongs, or lack of proper burial rites could tether a spirit to the roads and parishes she once walked.",
		"rank5_trivia": "Because documented historical records on the kaperosa are limited, details vary noticeably by town and province. While most modern retellings cast her in stark white, older regional accounts occasionally describe a dark or grey hooded figure, blurring the line between the kaperosa and other colonial-era church ghosts such as the headless priest (paring pugot).\n\nNot every sighting casts her as a malicious threat. In several Visayan and Tagalog oral tales, the kaperosa appears simply to lead a brave traveler toward an unmarked grave, silently asking for prayers or justice. By the mid-twentieth century, English-influenced urban legends—most famously the White Lady of Balete Drive in Quezon City—gradually eclipsed the older Spanish-derived name in Metro Manila.",
		"rank6_epilogue": "Today, the exact word \"kaperosa\" is heard less often in everyday city speech, surviving primarily among older regional speakers, Visayan storytellers, and Philippine folklore archives. Yet her unmistakable silhouette—the silent, pale-robed woman standing beside a darkened road—remains one of the most enduring icons of Filipino horror.\n\nHer legacy lives on through the nationwide White Lady tradition, featured in countless Undas ghost stories, classic television specials like Magandang Gabi... Bayan, and Philippine cinema franchises such as Shake, Rattle & Roll. In recent years, Filipino illustrators, researchers, and indie creators have increasingly revived the traditional name kaperosa to reconnect modern pop-culture ghosts with their regional roots."
	},
	"tk": {
		"creature": "Tikbalang",
		"region": "Luzon (Tagalog Regions & Rizal)",
		"portrait": "res://Assets/characters/portraits/TK Face.png",
		"rank1_origin": "The tikbalang (also spelled tigbalang or tigbalaang) is a tall, bony humanoid creature with the head and hooves of a horse and disproportionately long limbs, said to dwell in Philippine mountains, forests, and ancient trees. Its earliest known written mention appears in Fray Juan de Plasencia's Customs of the Tagalogs (1589), which briefly notes: \"There were also ghosts, which they called vibit; and phantoms, which they called Tigbalaang.\"\n\nUnlike predatory blood-drinkers such as the aswang, the tikbalang is primarily a trickster and territorial forest spirit. While notorious for confusing travelers in the woods, Tagalog folklore from Rizal Province also remembers the creature as a benevolent guardian of elemental kingdoms, standing watch beneath great trees against intruders who mean harm.",
		"rank2_taboos": "Rural etiquette emphasizes quiet respect when entering dense forests, bamboo groves, or the shadow of old balete and kalumpang trees. Travelers are advised not to make excessive noise and to ask permission aloud—often with \"Tabi-tabi po\"—to avoid offending a resident tikbalang. If the creature plays its signature prank of leading a traveler in endless circles, folk wisdom prescribes taking off one's shirt and wearing it inside out to break the spell.\n\nOral tradition also preserves a daring method to tame a tikbalang: a brave challenger must leap onto its back, bind it with a specially prepared cord, and hold on while it thrashes wildly through the air until exhausted. Plucking one of the three thickest spines from its mane yields an anting-anting (talisman) that binds the creature to serve its captor.",
		"rank3_depiction_path": "res://Assets/ui/journal/tikbalang.jpg",
		"rank3_depiction_orientation": "portrait",
		"rank3_depiction_source": "Artwork: \"Tikbalang\" by isaiahpaul (DeviantArt)",
		"rank3_depiction_url": "https://www.deviantart.com/isaiahpaul/art/Tikbalang-113487599",
		"rank3_body": "Oral lore describes the tikbalang as an extraordinarily tall, gaunt humanoid with the head and hind hooves of a horse, a mane of sharp spines, and limbs so long that its knees rise above its head whenever it squats. It favors dark, overgrown haunts—lurking beneath bridges, inside bamboo clumps and banana groves, or perched atop balete (Ficus) and kalumpang (Sterculia foetida) trees.\n\nCapable of turning invisible or shapeshifting, the tikbalang may appear as a tall, slim man wearing a salakot and chewing a bamboo twig, or even as a complete white horse—as in a coastal fisherman's account preserved by Damiana L. Eugenio (1987). Travelers usually recognize an encounter through sudden, inexplicable disorientation along familiar trails, eerie noises in the foliage, or the uncanny sense of being watched from the treeline.",
		"rank4_history": "Colonial records reveal that the tikbalang's equine image developed gradually—notably because horses were not native to the pre-colonial Philippines. After Plasencia's 1589 mention, Fray Pedro de San Buenaventura (1613) described a winged, foul-smelling phantom (\"Dizen que tiene alas y que lleva los hombres donde quiere\"), while Augustinian friar Tomás Ortiz (c. 1731) recorded a shapeshifter appearing \"in the shape of a black man ... an old man ... a horse, or of a monster.\"\n\nBy the mid-eighteenth century, equine traits solidified: Juan José Delgado (c. 1750) described a being with \"the mouth of a horse,\" and Antonio Mozo (1763) noted \"the body of a giant and the legs of a horse.\" Researchers have also compared the figure to the Hindu-Buddhist horse-headed deity Hayagrīva from regional Asian trade, though historians treat this as a plausible comparison rather than proven direct ancestry.",
		"rank5_trivia": "One of the most famous folk sayings linked to the creature explains sunshowers—rain falling under a bright, clear sky—with the phrase \"May kinakasal na tikbalang\" (\"A tikbalang is getting married\"), echoing similar trickster-wedding weather proverbs across Spain and Asia. Some colonial-era folk beliefs also claim a tikbalang is the transformation of an aborted fetus sent back to earth from limbo.\n\nRather than a single uniform nationwide myth, ethnographic records highlight distinct regional parallels. Folklorist Maximo D. Ramos and anthropologist Robert B. Fox documented the Mount Pinatubo Aeta's tulung—a horse-like spirit with clawed feet and long hair—and Baler's fiery-backed binangunan, while Delgado noted Visayans equating the tikbalang with the unglo.",
		"rank6_epilogue": "Today, the tikbalang stands among the most recognizable icons of Philippine pop culture, appearing across komiks, film, and television—from classic fantasy episodes of Da Adventures of Pedro Penduko (2006) to Budjette Tan and Kajo Baldisimo's graphic novel and Netflix series Trese, which reimagines tikbalang clans as sleek, powerful underworld figures in modern Manila. Its name has even reached international sci-fi, inspiring the \"Tickbalang\" mobile-suit flight carrier in Mobile Suit Gundam: The Witch from Mercury (2022).\n\nIn everyday Filipino life, the creature endures both in lighthearted remarks whenever rain falls on a sunny afternoon and in a quiet, lasting respect for the country's remaining old-growth trees and mountain trails."
	},
	"buboy": {
		"creature": "Bungisngis",
		"region": "Luzon (Batangas & Bataan)",
		"portrait": "res://Assets/characters/portraits/Buboy Face.png",
		"rank1_origin": "The bungisngis (historically spelled Buñgisñgis) is a forest-dwelling giant of Tagalog folklore, famous for his booming, incessant laughter and immense physical strength. Written records of the creature are relatively sparse compared to nationwide figures like the aswang or kapre; the earliest major documented account appears in Dean S. Fansler's Filipino Popular Tales (1921), where Batangas narrator José M. Hilario defined the bungisngis as \"a large strong man that is always laughing.\"\n\nIn oral lore, the bungisngis dwells deep in the woods, drawn out of hiding by the sound and aroma of cooking food. Rather than a ritualized nature deity, he functions as a classic folktale ogre—formidable in brute strength yet notoriously gullible, vain, and easily undone by a clever adversary.",
		"rank2_taboos": "Unlike spirits such as the kapre or nuno sa punso, documented folklore records no formal offerings, prayers, or ritual taboos like \"tabi-tabi po\" specifically directed at the bungisngis. Instead, oral tradition treats encounters with him as tests of wit and solidarity rather than spiritual etiquette.\n\nFolk wisdom drawn from his best-known tale emphasizes two practical defenses: never confront him alone, and never rely on brute force. In Fansler's 1921 Batangas account, the giant refuses to ambush a group together, noting that \"he did not dare attack all three of them at once, for in union there is strength.\" When cornered solo, quick thinking, flattery, and misdirection are the surest way to outmaneuver him, as the bungisngis panics easily when caught off guard.",
		"rank3_depiction_path": "res://Assets/ui/journal/bungisngis.jpg",
		"rank3_depiction_orientation": "portrait",
		"rank3_depiction_source": "Artwork: \"Bungisngis\" by isaiahpaul (DeviantArt)",
		"rank3_depiction_url": "https://www.deviantart.com/isaiahpaul/art/Bungisngis-113488558",
		"rank3_body": "Traditional descriptions paint a grotesque, towering humanoid of herculean build. Fansler's 1921 collection records that \"The Buñgisñgis can lift a huge animal as easily as if it were a feather,\" noting his large, permanently exposed teeth and an enormous upper lip that covers his entire face when thrown back. Later twentieth-century folklore compiled by Maximo D. Ramos (1971) adds a single eye in the center of his forehead—compensated by an acute sense of hearing—and two long tusks jutting from the sides of his mouth.\n\nEncountered in remote forests and rural clearings, the bungisngis announces his presence through constant giggling or laughter and a bold demand for whatever meal is on the fire. Though strong enough to hurl a full-grown carabao knee-deep into the earth, his short temper and curiosity repeatedly betray him.",
		"rank4_history": "The creature's name stems from the Tagalog root ngisi (\"to show the teeth\"), closely mirrored by the Cebuano ngisi (\"to grin\" or \"giggle\"), capturing his defining habit of constantly baring his teeth in laughter. While modern pop culture frequently dubs him the \"Philippine Cyclops,\" historical records show that his earliest 1921 description mentioned only his massive lip, exposed teeth, and strength—with the single forehead eye and tusks appearing in later regional accounts recorded by Maximo Ramos (1971).\n\nComparative folklore points to deep pre-colonial Southeast Asian roots. Fansler (1921) noted striking structural parallels between the Tagalog bungisngis tale and Borneo folktales of the clever mouse-deer (plandok) outwitting a food-stealing forest giant, situating the bungisngis within a shared regional tradition of trickster-versus-ogre storytelling.",
		"rank5_trivia": "Nearly everything known about the bungisngis's personality comes from a single celebrated Tagalog tale collected by Fansler: \"The Three Friends—The Monkey, the Dog, and the Carabao.\" After the giant plants the carabao knee-deep in the ground and batters the dog, the clever monkey outwits him three times—trapping him in a pit, tricking him into pulling a beehive disguised as \"the king's bell,\" and finally persuading him to wear a live boa constrictor as a royal belt.\n\nRegional records also show close kinship with neighboring Luzon giant lore. In Pampanga, Fansler's companion tale \"Suac and His Adventures\" features a nearly identical food-stealing forest giant named Pugut, illustrating how similar ogre motifs traveled across ethno-linguistic borders under different local names.",
		"rank6_epilogue": "In everyday Filipino speech, the word bungisngis survives far beyond folklore as a lively Tagalog term for giggling or chuckling—a playful linguistic echo of the laughing giant. Though less universally known than the tikbalang or manananggal, the creature has enjoyed a steady revival in modern Philippine pop culture.\n\nHe appeared on television in Komiks Presents: Da Adventures of Pedro Penduko (2006, portrayed by Chokoleit), and has been reimagined in contemporary Philippine speculative fiction—from Budjette Tan and Kajo Baldisimo's Trese universe, where bungisngis serve as towering, one-eyed underworld bodyguards, to Edgar Calabia Samar's Janus Silang novels, keeping this centuries-old Tagalog forest giant alive for a new generation of readers."
	},
	"sarimanok": {
		"creature": "Sarimanok",
		"region": "Mindanao (Lake Lanao, Maranao)",
		"portrait": "res://Assets/characters/portraits/Sarimanok Face.png",
		"rank1_origin": "The sarimanok is the legendary, multicolored bird of the Maranao people of Lake Lanao and one of the most celebrated emblems of Philippine indigenous art. Revered as a symbol of good fortune, prosperity, and prestige, it stands apart from frightening folk creatures like the aswang as a cherished cultural and artistic icon.\n\nMaranao oral tradition traces the sarimanok to an ancient totem bird called the itotoro, believed to act as a bridge between the human realm and the spirit world through its unseen twin spirit bird, the inikadowa. Across generations, this mythic fowl became the signature expression of Maranao craftsmanship and royal identity.",
		"rank2_taboos": "Because the sarimanok is a royal and cultural emblem rather than a feared forest spirit, traditional etiquette governs how it is displayed and respected. According to Maranao custom, the sarimanok is never shown entirely on its own; in ceremonial settings, it is traditionally accompanied by a formal ensemble of flags, banners, and standards (panji), or mounted proudly atop the ceremonial umbrella of a Sultan or dignitary.\n\nAlthough non-traditional uses have expanded over time—such as Mindanao State University adopting the bird for graduation exercises—Maranao cultural stewards emphasize treating the motif with dignity. Honoring its roots means recognizing its living connection to Lake Lanao heritage rather than reducing it to a generic decorative ornament.",
		"rank3_depiction_path": "res://Assets/ui/journal/sarimanok.jpg",
		"rank3_depiction_orientation": "portrait",
		"rank3_depiction_source": "Artwork: \"Sarimanok VII\" by Nihaya P. Tamano (Filipino Artist Group, Facebook)",
		"rank3_depiction_url": "https://www.facebook.com/groups/filipinoartist/posts/1814454898947793/",
		"rank3_body": "Traditional Maranao lore and craft depict the sarimanok as a resplendent, stylized fowl with vibrantly colored wings and a sweeping feathered tail. Its head and plumage are intricately embellished with okir—the flowing scroll, leaf, and spiral motifs central to Maranao art. Its most unmistakable trait is a fish held firmly in its beak or talons, a detail linked both to abundance and to ritual food offerings for the spirit world.\n\nRather than a physical beast encountered along forest trails, the sarimanok is recognized through ancestral storytelling, carved wooden totems, and ceremonial regalia. Its presence in a gathering signals celebration, noble lineage, and the hope of good fortune.",
		"rank4_history": "The name \"sarimanok\" combines sari (\"assorted,\" \"various,\" or colorful cloth) with manok—a word that originally meant \"bird\" in early Philippine languages before narrowing to \"chicken\"—effectively describing a bird of assorted colors. Anthropologist Jesus T. Peralta (1980) noted that while Islam discourages literal representations of living creatures, pre-Islamic indigenous motifs survived in Maranao art by evolving into highly stylized, curvilinear okir abstractions.\n\nAlongside its pre-Islamic roots as the itotoro totem, later Islamic folklore in southern Mindanao enriched the bird's prestige through the legend of a colossal celestial rooster seen by the Prophet Muhammad in the first of the seven heavens—so vast its crest touched the second heaven, its crowing rousing every living creature except humans until the Day of Resurrection.",
		"rank5_trivia": "In traditional Maranao design terminology documented by anthropologist Jesus T. Peralta (1980), artistic motifs carry distinct gendered pairs: sarimanok refers to the male form, while its feminine counterpart is known as the papanok. Most traditional carved sarimanok figures originate from Tugaya, Lanao del Sur—widely regarded as the historic artisan capital of Maranao woodcarving and brasswork.\n\nMaranao oral storytelling also preserves competing origin legends. One recounts a glowing rooster that transformed into a handsome prince and carried away Sari, the beloved daughter of the Sultan of Lanao, prompting the grieving father to commission carvings in her memory. Another tells of Raja Indarapatra carving a wooden rooster that came to life to carry him to the moon goddess he loved.",
		"rank6_epilogue": "From its origins around Lake Lanao, the sarimanok has grown into a nationwide emblem of Filipino artistry. National Artist for Visual Arts Abdulmari Asia Imao (1936–2014)—the Philippines' first Moro National Artist—played a pivotal role in bringing the sarimanok, alongside okir and naga motifs, into mainstream contemporary Philippine painting and sculpture.\n\nThe bird's silhouette is equally familiar in modern media and public life. Mindanao State University adopted Sari the Sarimanok as its official mascot, while ABS-CBN famously used the bird for its 1966 color broadcasts, its 1993 Gold Medal-winning Sarimanok Station ID, and the Sarimanok News Channel (1996–1999). Today, it continues to inspire new generations, from community murals to P-pop group Bini's Sarimanok-inspired stage attire at Summer Sonic 2026."
	},
	"danilo": {
		"creature": "Daligmata",
		"region": "Eastern Visayas & Northeastern Mindanao",
		"portrait": "res://Assets/characters/portraits/Danilo Face.png",
		"rank1_origin": "Written records of the daligmata are sparse and strikingly varied, revealing a name shared by distinct traditions across the Visayas and Mindanao. In nineteenth- and early twentieth-century Eastern Visayan lexicography, Antonio Sánchez de la Rosa's Diccionario bisaya-español para las provincias de Sámar y Leyte (1914) defines it as a nocturnal, many-eyed creature or sorcerer: \"Brujo, animal nocturno, que, según dicen los bisayas, está lleno de ojos resplandecientes.\"\n\nIn Northeastern Mindanao, however, the daligmata is neither a monster nor a witch. As documented by ethnomusicologist José Semblante Buenconsejo (2002), Agusan Manobo communities honor the daligmata as an enigmatic spirit helper (abyan)—a sharp-sighted ally called upon during healing rituals to rescue lost human souls.",
		"rank2_taboos": "Because written accounts of the Visayan nocturnal daligmata are brief, no formal household taboos or protective charms like garlic or salt are recorded specifically against it. Instead, documented folk practice centers on the Mindanao healing tradition, where engaging the daligmata belongs strictly to trained ritual specialists rather than ordinary villagers.\n\nIn Agusan Manobo cosmology, when a person falls gravely ill because their soul has been abducted, a spirit medium conducts the gudguden ceremony (Buenconsejo, 2002). Respectfully summoned through ritual chant and spirit possession, the daligmata works alongside the medium's personal guardian spirits (abyan), lending its supernatural sight to trace the missing soul across unseen realms and guide it safely back to the patient's body.",
		"rank3_depiction_path": "res://Assets/ui/journal/daligmata.jpg",
		"rank3_depiction_orientation": "portrait",
		"rank3_depiction_source": "Artwork: \"Daligmata\" by rueshia (DeviantArt)",
		"rank3_depiction_url": "https://www.deviantart.com/rueshia/art/Daligmata-939688854",
		"rank3_body": "Eastern Visayan oral tradition preserved by Sánchez de la Rosa (1914) portrays the daligmata as a mysterious nocturnal animal—or shapeshifting sorcerer—whose body is covered in brilliant, shining eyes. Appearing only under cover of darkness, it is recognized by the eerie shimmer of countless glowing eyes peering out of the night.\n\nIn Agusan Manobo lore, the daligmata is far less physically defined, known primarily through its extraordinary perceptual power rather than a fixed animal form. Associated with \"fast vision,\" it navigates the spirit plane with unmatched clarity, spotting hidden entities, lost objects, and wandering souls that remain invisible to mortal eyes.",
		"rank4_history": "The name \"daligmata\" draws on the widespread Philippine root mata (\"eye\"), paired with a prefix connoting swiftness—translated in Agusan Manobo ethnography as \"fast vision\" (Buenconsejo, 2002). This shared linguistic core explains why the term appears across centuries and islands in connection with extraordinary sight, from piercing the dark to seeing through supernatural concealment.\n\nLong before modern folklore compendiums, seventeenth-century Jesuit chronicler Fray Francisco Ignacio Alcina's Historia de las islas e indios de Bisayas (1668) linked the term daligmata to Visayan magical botany—specifically as a rare herb or charm used to spot sorcerers who had rendered themselves invisible. Over time, the word came to bridge three intertwined ideas: a sight-granting plant, a many-eyed night creature, and a keen-eyed spirit guide.",
		"rank5_trivia": "Folklorists and researchers highlight that \"daligmata\" in the Visayas was not always a creature at all. Alongside Alcina's 1668 account, the University of Manila Journal of East Asiatic Studies (1956) likewise records daligmata among the Visayans as a rare herb enabling its bearer to see witches who have made themselves invisible—though its exact botanical identity remains uncertain in modern records.\n\nModern readers also frequently compare the daligmata to Dalikmata (or Dalikamata), a separate many-eyed Visayan diwata traditionally invoked to cure eye ailments. While both names share the root mata and vivid ocular imagery, ethnographic records keep their roles distinct: Dalikmata as a healing deity of the eyes, and Daligmata as a nocturnal many-eyed being, herb of true sight, or Manobo soul-finding spirit.",
		"rank6_epilogue": "Unlike nationwide icons such as the kapre or manananggal, the daligmata remained largely tucked away inside Spanish-era dictionaries and academic ethnographies for much of the twentieth century. Its sparse historical footprint kept it out of classic Filipino horror cinema, leaving its oldest regional nuances known mainly to linguists, anthropologists, and local elders.\n\nIn recent years, however, the daligmata has experienced a quiet cultural revival. Digital folklore archives such as The Aswang Project, independent Filipino illustrators, and contemporary speculative fiction writers have brought its striking \"thousand shining eyes\" motif to new audiences—reintroducing both the mysterious Visayan night creature and the benevolent Manobo spirit helper to a wider generation."
	},
	"brahim": {
		"creature": "Albularyo",
		"region": "Nationwide (Luzon, Visayas & Mindanao)",
		"portrait": "res://Assets/characters/portraits/Brahim Face.png",
		"rank1_origin": "Unlike mythical figures such as the kapre or aswang, the albularyo (also spelled arbularyo) is a real, living cultural practitioner: the traditional Filipino folk healer and herbalist. Anthropologists such as F. Landa Jocano and Michael L. Tan describe the albularyo as the \"general practitioner\" of rural Philippine healthcare, bridging physical medicine with spiritual care.\n\nAcross the archipelago, these healers go by many regional names—including mananambal in Cebu, Bohol, and Siquijor, tambalan in Samar and Leyte, and parabulong in Bicol. Whether treating common fevers with boiled leaves or diagnosing folk ailments like usog and spirit-caused illness, the albularyo remains a trusted neighborhood fixture in communities where ancestral worldview and daily health intertwine.",
		"rank2_taboos": "Traditional ethics sit at the heart of the albularyo's calling. Most practitioners regard their healing skill not as a commercial trade, but as a sacred vocation or God-given gift passed down through family lineage, apprenticeship, or a profound spiritual experience. Because of this belief, many traditional healers refuse to charge fixed fees, accepting only voluntary donations (donasyon or alay) out of conviction that profiting from their craft could cause them to lose their power.\n\nRespectful etiquette also guides every consultation. Patients approach the healer with humility and honesty, while communities draw a sharp moral line between ethical healers—who vow to mend body and spirit—and sorcerers (mangkukulam) accused of inflicting harm.",
		"rank3_depiction_path": "res://Assets/ui/journal/albularyo.jpg",
		"rank3_depiction_orientation": "portrait",
		"rank3_depiction_source": "Artwork: \"What is an Albularyo?\" by Chae Won Cha (Facebook)",
		"rank3_depiction_url": "https://www.facebook.com/chaewon.cha.9",
		"rank3_body": "An albularyo wears no theatrical costume; in everyday life, they are ordinary neighbors—often respected elders—who receive visitors in modest homes adorned with Catholic icons, dried roots, and bottles of infused coconut oil (lana). Their craft treats the whole person, addressing physical symptoms alongside emotional and spiritual imbalance.\n\nConsultations typically begin with feeling the wrist pulse (pagpupulso) or performing pagtatawas—reading patterns formed by melted candle wax or alum dropped into water—to identify whether an ailment is natural or spirit-induced. Treatment blends herbal decoctions, poultices, smoke baths (suob or palina), and gentle massage with whispered prayers (orasyon) drawn from handwritten booklets called librito, pairing ancestral botany with deeply personal faith.",
		"rank4_history": "The word \"albularyo\" derives from the Spanish herbolario (\"herbalist\"), reflecting centuries of cultural adaptation. Before Spanish colonization, indigenous communities relied on revered spiritual leaders and healers—the babaylan of the Visayas and katalonan of Luzon—who mediated between the human and spirit worlds. When colonial authorities suppressed indigenous priesthoods, folk healing endured by weaving pre-colonial plant knowledge and animist cosmology together with Catholic saints, prayers, and Latin-derived incantations.\n\nEven nineteenth-century physicians recognized the empirical value of this tradition. In Plantas Medicinales de Filipinas (1892), Dr. Trinidad Pardo de Tavera cataloged over 200 local medicinal plants used by Filipino herbolarios, cautioning doctors that ideas dismissed as superstition were often \"fundamentally based on observed events.\"",
		"rank5_trivia": "Anthropologist Michael L. Tan has noted that calling an albularyo an \"herbalist\" is actually somewhat misleading in the Philippine context: rather than relying solely on small garden herbs, Filipino folk healers draw from the bark, roots, resins, and leaves of towering forest trees. In rural communities, the albularyo also rarely works in isolation, often referring patients to specialized peers such as the manghihilot (traditional bone-setter and midwife), the mangluluop (ritual diviner), or the mediko, who blends folk remedies with modern pharmaceuticals.\n\nIn provinces like Siquijor and parts of the Visayas, Holy Week—especially Good Friday—remains a sacred peak in the healing calendar, when practitioners venture into caves and forests for pangalap, gathering wild roots and brewing medicinal oils believed to reach their highest potency.",
		"rank6_epilogue": "Today, the albularyo continues to play a vital role across geographically isolated and low-income communities, while also attracting urban Filipinos seeking holistic care. Rather than vanishing under modernization, traditional healing has gained formal national recognition: Republic Act No. 8423—the Traditional and Alternative Medicine Act (TAMA) of 1997, authored by Senator Juan M. Flavier—established the Philippine Institute of Traditional and Alternative Health Care (PITAHC) under the Department of Health to study and integrate safe traditional practices into national healthcare.\n\nLong-standing folk remedies such as lagundi, sambong, and bayabas have since been scientifically validated and widely adopted, as contemporary Filipino physicians, anthropologists, and cultural stewards increasingly champion respectful collaboration between modern medicine and ancestral healing heritage."
	},
	"rodel": {
		"creature": "Litao",
		"region": "Ilocos Region (Vigan, Ilocos Sur)",
		"portrait": "res://Assets/characters/portraits/Rodel Face.png",
		"rank1_origin": "The litao is a little-known male water anito (nature spirit) of Ilokano folklore, tied to rivers, seas, and the swaying bamboo groves that line their banks. Written historical records of the litao are sparse, resting primarily on pioneer folklorist Isabelo de los Reyes's El Folk-Lore Filipino (1889), where he proposed that the litao—not the mermaid—was the original indigenous water spirit: \"Probablemente Litao fué el anito del mar y de los ríos, y nó la Sirena\" (\"Probably Litao was the anito of the sea and rivers, and not the Sirena\").\n\nIn the oral traditions of Vigan, Ilocos Sur, the litao is remembered as the husband of the sirena and the very source of her supernatural power—a quiet guardian of waterways rather than a bloodthirsty sea monster.",
		"rank2_taboos": "Because written accounts dedicated solely to the litao are brief, traditional precautions surrounding him draw directly from Ilokano river- and tree-spirit etiquette. Local lore warns against cutting down bamboo clumps growing along riverbanks where a litao dwells, as disturbing his home is believed to provoke sudden illness or misfortune.\n\nAcross the Ilocos Region, travelers entering unfamiliar waterways, groves, or spirit-inhabited places traditionally murmur \"Dayu-dayu, bari-bari\" to ask respectful passage from unseen anitos. When someone falls inexplicably sick after disturbing a riverbank or wooded spot, families have long turned to atang—ritual food offerings such as rice cakes—to appease the offended spirit and restore harmony.",
		"rank3_depiction_path": "res://Assets/ui/journal/litao.jpg",
		"rank3_depiction_orientation": "portrait",
		"rank3_depiction_source": "Artwork: \"Litao\" by emirajuju (Instagram)",
		"rank3_depiction_url": "https://www.instagram.com/emirajuju/",
		"rank3_body": "Unlike the fish-tailed mermen of European lore, Vigan oral tradition recorded by Isabelo de los Reyes (1889) describes the litao as a small, human-like male (\"un varón pequeño\") who makes his home high among the branches of bamboo trees along riverbanks. Rather than lurking exclusively beneath the waves, he watches over the threshold where land and water meet.\n\nFolk accounts add that when a litao takes ordinary human form, his true nature can be betrayed by a strong, foul odor. Encounters are rarely marked by open attacks; instead, villagers recognize his presence through the eerie stillness of riverside bamboo, unexplained ailments following careless tree-cutting, or tales of a mysterious benefactor who grants aquatic power and immortality to his mermaid bride.",
		"rank4_history": "Linguists trace the word \"litaw\" across Philippine languages to the Proto-Philippine root *letaw (\"to float,\" from Proto-Austronesian *-taw), a meaning that also carries the sense of surfacing or emerging into view—a fitting name for a spirit of rivers and seas. Early philological notes also remarked that the word litaw itself leaned closer to Tagalog usage than Ilokano, hinting at older cross-regional ties.\n\nHistorians and folklorists view the litao as a striking example of colonial-era syncretism. As Isabelo de los Reyes (1889) observed, Spanish settlers introduced the European concept of the sirena (mermaid) to the archipelago. Rather than vanishing, the older Ilokano water anito adapted to the new storytelling landscape—reimagined as the sirena's husband and the true origin of her magic.",
		"rank5_trivia": "One enduring Ilokano legend retold in modern folklore archives explains the birth of mermaids through the romance between the first litao and a mortal maiden named Serena. Captivated by her singing along the shore, the water spirit married her, divided the sea, and granted her immortality in an underwater palace—yet over generations, mortals remembered Serena and nearly forgot her diminutive husband.\n\nIn older regional usage, the term litao occasionally overlapped with sirena or duyong (dugong), showing how aquatic spirit names shifted over time. Neighboring Ilokano lore preserves close kin to this bamboo-dwelling spirit, notably the kaibaan (kibaan) and ansisit—small, territorial nature spirits who likewise punish careless trespassers with illness.",
		"rank6_epilogue": "Overshadowed early on by the far more famous sirena and siyokoy, the litao stayed out of twentieth-century Philippine cinema and mainstream television, surviving mainly in Isabelo de los Reyes's 1889 landmark ethnography and regional Ilokano memory.\n\nIn recent years, however, digital folklore archives and contemporary Filipino creators have brought the litao back into the spotlight. Featured in Karl Gaverza's Philippine Spirits series, The Aswang Project, independent illustrations, and online Filipino folklore storytelling, this quiet bamboo-dwelling water anito is being rediscovered as a rare glimpse into pre-colonial Ilokano water beliefs before European mermaids reached Philippine shores."
	},
	"lolopilo": {
		"creature": "Duwende",
		"region": "Nationwide (Luzon, Visayas & Mindanao)",
		"portrait": "res://Assets/characters/portraits/LoloPilo Face.png",
		"rank1_origin": "The duwende is one of the Philippines' most familiar household and earth spirits: a diminutive, gnome-like being said to dwell in termite mounds, old trees, caves, and dark, unvisited corners of homes. Across Christianized lowland communities, the name is closely intertwined with the Tagalog nuno sa punso (\"old man of the mound\") and other small, land-dwelling spirits.\n\nUnlike predatory monsters such as the aswang, the duwende is neither inherently evil nor purely benevolent. Oral traditions compiled by folklorists Maximo D. Ramos and Francisco R. Demetrio portray these little folk as fiercely reciprocal neighbors—capable of granting good fortune to respectful or vulnerable humans, yet swift to inflict mischief or sudden illness on anyone who trespasses on their territory.",
		"rank2_taboos": "Everyday Filipino etiquette toward the duwende centers on courtesy toward unseen spaces. When passing termite mounds, large trees, or quiet backyards at dusk, people traditionally murmur \"Tabi-tabi po\" (\"Excuse me, please\") to ask permission before crossing. Children are taught never to point directly at an anthill or mound—and to lightly bite their own finger if they forget—to avoid provoking the spirit inside.\n\nDestroying a mound or urinating near a suspected dwelling without asking leave is considered a serious taboo, believed to trigger unexplained fevers, swelling, or persistent bad luck. When an offense occurs, families have traditionally offered small platters of food or sweets and sought folk healers to appease the angered spirit.",
		"rank3_depiction_path": "res://Assets/ui/journal/duwende.jpg",
		"rank3_depiction_orientation": "portrait",
		"rank3_depiction_source": "Artwork: \"Matanda sa Punso\" by isaiahpaul (DeviantArt)",
		"rank3_depiction_url": "https://www.deviantart.com/isaiahpaul/art/Matanda-sa-Punso-113487762",
		"rank3_body": "Oral lore commonly describes the duwende as tiny—roughly the height of a small child—yet often bearing the weathered face and long beard of an old man. They inhabit earth mounds, rocky crevices, hollow trees, and rarely disturbed nooks inside houses, where they remain invisible to most adults while reportedly showing themselves more readily to young children.\n\nPeople typically recognize a duwende's presence through subtle domestic signs rather than dramatic sightings: small objects vanishing and reappearing in plain sight, faint footsteps or chuckling in quiet rooms, or a child claiming to play with an unseen friend. While playful duwende merely tease residents, offended ones are blamed for sudden aches, fevers, or recurring household mishaps.",
		"rank4_history": "The word \"duwende\" entered Philippine languages from the Spanish duende, itself a contraction of duen de casa (from dueño de casa, meaning \"master\" or \"owner of the house\"). In El Folk-Lore Filipino (1889), pioneer folklorist Isabelo de los Reyes explicitly remarked on this colonial introduction: \"El duende es uno de los séres mitológicos introducidos en Filipinas por los españoles, á juzgar porque hasta su nombre exótico se conserva\" (\"The duende is one of the mythological beings introduced to the Philippines by the Spaniards, judging by the fact that even its foreign name is preserved\").\n\nOver centuries, this Spanish umbrella term merged with much older indigenous beliefs in earth- and mound-dwelling nature spirits, blending European household-goblin imagery with pre-colonial reverence for ancestral land and unseen ecological guardians.",
		"rank5_trivia": "While modern pop culture often divides them neatly into \"good white duwende\" and \"bad black duwende,\" legends preserved by Damiana L. Eugenio—including \"The Duwendes at Lopez Elementary School\"—reveal a more nuanced tradition where human conduct, gratitude, and kept secrets matter far more than color. In the Ilokano tradition, the closely related ansisit is even said to prefer farmers who plow with a carabao rather than a tractor, fearing heavy machinery will crush its underground home.\n\nFolklorists Maximo D. Ramos and Francisco R. Demetrio also document a rich mosaic of regional little folk under distinct local names: the Tagalog matanda sa punso and taong-lupa, the Hiligaynon kama-kama, the Ifugao kalanget, the Ilongot lampong, and the Ibanag aran.",
		"rank6_epilogue": "Few figures in Philippine folklore remain as woven into daily life as the duwende. Across rural provinces and bustling cities alike, \"Tabi-tabi po\" survives as an instinctive expression of courtesy, and families still half-jokingly blame a playful duwende whenever keys, eyeglasses, or small household items mysteriously go missing.\n\nThe creature has also thrived in contemporary Philippine television and comics. GMA Network's fantasy series Dwarfina (2011) brought diminutive folk into primetime drama, while Budjette Tan and Kajo Baldisimo's graphic novel and Netflix series Trese cleverly subverted the long-standing showbiz rumor of celebrities keeping a lucky duwende—reimagining them as sharp-witted underworld patrons who trade favors for loyalty and sweets."
	}
}

static func get_entry(clean_id: String) -> Dictionary:
